course: dictionary
lesson: 13965
title: Limping: when walking goes wrong
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words paint a picture as well as giving a meaning. Limping is one of them. «Sam fell off his bike and came limping home.» You can see Sam walking unevenly, with one leg hurting, so limping means walking unevenly because a leg or foot hurts.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «Sam's ankle was sore, so he was limping badly.» The word sore tells you why he limps. A limping character in a story is usually hurt or very tired. Ask yourself: what is the writer trying to show me?
explain: Limping can also describe things that go on slowly and badly. «The club was limping along, with no money left.» This is not about feet at all. Limping along means carrying on, but only just and not very well.
fact: Limping means walking unevenly, often because a leg or foot hurts.
fact: «Limping» can be a verb («She is limping»), an adjective («a limping dog») or a noun («limping»).
fact: «Limping badly» means walking with a very uneven step, which is painful or hard to do.
fact: If you see a child limping while playing, they should stop and tell an adult.
fact: «Limping along» can mean going on slowly and badly, as with a limping business or plan.
exercise: «The dog was limping after running on the stones.» What does limping mean? | Running very fast in a straight line | *Walking unevenly because a leg or foot hurts | Sleeping in the sun | Barking loudly at strangers
exercise: «Ella came limping back to the car.» Find and copy the one word that tells you how Ella walked. | car | *limping | back | came
exercise: «The plan was limping along with no money left.» What does limping along suggest? | Walking very quickly | Flying high and fast | Singing happily | *Going on slowly and badly
test: A child is limping in the playground. What should they do? | Keep playing until the bell rings | Run faster to show they are fine | Hide the hurt and walk home alone | *Stop playing and tell an adult
test: Which sentence uses limping as an adjective? | «He was limping across the yard.» | *«The limping dog waited by the gate.» | «They are limping to school now.»
exam: In «The team was limping through the season with no wins», what does limping mean? | *Going on slowly and badly | Jumping and winning | Walking unevenly on a sore foot | Eating lunch together
